@meta
id: 07-04
title: Drill — five numbers, five jumps
chapter: 07
type: drill
skills: goto-line
par: 40
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: Ctrl-G, type, Enter, click, type — five times over
open: default.json

@brief
`jq` refuses this 74 line error registry and lists the lines: 13, 29, 41,
57, 69. Each one is a closing brace that needs a trailing comma.

Nothing to work out. Read a number, jump to it, append a comma, read the
next number. The dojo will hand you the file eight times.

@keys
| `13G` | line 13 |
| `A` | append at the end of the line |
| `Esc` | back to normal mode |

@hints
The whole rep is number, `G`, `A`, comma, `Esc`. Five of those and you are
done.
--
Do not check where you are between jumps. `nG` does not care where you
were, so looking costs you time and buys you nothing.

@solution
    13G A , <Esc>  29G A , <Esc>  41G A , <Esc>  57G A , <Esc>  69G A , <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Thirty-four keystrokes for five fixes in a file you never scrolled.

The reason this is worth drilling rather than just knowing is the gap
between reading a number and your fingers being at that line. Fluent, it is
under a second and you keep the next number in your head while it happens.
Hesitant, you lose the list and start reaching for the mouse to keep your
place.

Two digits then `G`. That is all it ever is, whether the file is eighty
lines or eight thousand.

